/* book Worked example 2: Q: A free beam has four equal booms A at (±200, ±75) mm, E = 70 GPa, α = 23 × 10⁻⁶ per K. Boom 1 at (200, 75), next to a hot duct, warms by 50 K; the others stay cold. Find the boom stresses.
   1. E α T₁ = 70,000 × 23 × 10⁻⁶ × 50 = 80.5 N/mm². Call it s, so NT = s A, MxT = s A × 75 and MyT = s A × 200.
   2. Section: total area 4A, Ixx = 4A × 75², Iyy = 4A × 200², Ixy = 0.
   3. Terms at boom i: NT/4A = s/4; MxT yi/Ixx = s yi/(4 × 75); MyT xi/Iyy = s xi/(4 × 200).
   4. Boom 1 (200, 75): σ = s(−1 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4) = −s/4 = −20.1 MPa.
   5. Boom 2 (−200, 75): σ = s(1/4 + 1/4 − 1/4) = +20.1 MPa. Boom 3 (−200, −75): s(1/4 − 1/4 − 1/4) = −20.1 MPa. Boom 4 (200, −75): +20.1 MPa.
   6. Checks: the four stresses sum to zero (no force), and Σσ y and Σσ x are both zero (no moments): the stress is self-equilibrating.
   Answer: ±20.1 MPa in a chequer pattern: the heated boom and the boom diagonally opposite in compression, the other two in tension. The beam also bends and stretches freely. */
